int Square(int x)          // returns an int; takes one int, called x inside
{
    return x * x;
}

void Greet(string name)    // void: returns nothing
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Hello, {name}!");
}
```

`return` hands a value back to whoever called the method. `void` means the method returns nothing. In a top-level program you can declare methods among your statements and call them before or after the declaration; inside a class (later in this course) they work the same way.

