steps.body:
A list finds things by position. A **dictionary** finds them by a **key** you choose, such as a name, and it stays fast even with millions of entries:

```csharp
var prices = new Dictionary<string, int>
{
    ["potion"] = 4,
    ["sword"] = 30,
};
Console.WriteLine(prices["sword"]);      // 30: look a value up by its key
prices["shield"] = 12;                   // a new key is added
prices["potion"] = 5;                    // an existing key gets a new value
Console.WriteLine(prices.Count);         // 3
Console.WriteLine(prices.ContainsKey("bow"));   // False

foreach (var (item, price) in prices)    // each entry is a key and a value
{
    Console.WriteLine($"{item}: {price}");
}
```

Each key appears at most once. Assigning to a key that exists replaces its value. Don't rely on the order a dictionary lists its entries in; if order matters, sort them or use a list.

