steps.body:
A **class** describes a kind of thing: the data it carries (**fields**) and what it can do (**methods**). `new` builds an **object** from it and runs the **constructor**, a special method with the class's name and no return type:

```csharp
var hero = new Player("Ana", 3);   // new runs the constructor and makes an object
hero.TakeHit();
Console.WriteLine($"{hero.Name}: {hero.Lives}");   // Ana: 2

class Player
{
    public string Name;      // fields: the data each Player object carries
    public int Lives;

    public Player(string name, int lives)   // constructor: same name as the class
    {
        Name = name;
        Lives = lives;
    }

    public void TakeHit()    // a method that works on this object's fields
    {
        Lives--;
    }
}
```

In a top-level program, classes go **after** the statements. Inside the class, `Lives` means *this* object's `Lives`; you can also write `this.Lives`.

