steps.body:
Public fields let any code set any value, even nonsense like `Lives = -50`. C# code uses **properties** instead: they read like fields, but you decide who can read and who can write:

```csharp
var hero = new Hero("Ana");
hero.TakeHit(30);
Console.WriteLine($"{hero.Name} {hero.Health} {hero.IsAlive}");   // Ana 70 True
// hero.Health = 100;    // would not compile: the setter is private

class Hero
{
    public string Name { get; }                      // set only by the constructor
    public int Health { get; private set; } = 100;   // anyone reads, only Hero writes
    public bool IsAlive => Health > 0;               // computed each time it's read

    public Hero(string name) => Name = name;

    public void TakeHit(int damage) => Health -= damage;
}
```

- `{ get; }`: read-only, set in the constructor
- `{ get; private set; }`: everyone reads, only the class writes
- `{ get; init; }`: set when the object is created, then fixed
- `=> expression`: computed every time it's read

Property names use PascalCase, like methods. (Java writes `getHealth()`; C# writes `Health`.)

