steps.body:
An **interface** lists members without code; a class that implements it promises to provide them. Unrelated classes can share an interface, and a class can implement **many**:

```csharp
IDamageable[] targets = { new Crate(), new Player() };
foreach (IDamageable t in targets)
{
    t.TakeDamage(5);
}

interface IDamageable
{
    void TakeDamage(int amount);   // no body: just the promise
}

class Crate : IDamageable
{
    public void TakeDamage(int amount) => Console.WriteLine("Crate breaks");
}

class Player : IDamageable
{
    public int Health = 20;

    public void TakeDamage(int amount)
    {
        Health -= amount;
        Console.WriteLine($"Player has {Health} HP");
    }
}
```

Code that only needs "something that can take damage" asks for an `IDamageable` and works with crates, players and anything added later. Interface names start with `I` by convention.

