steps.body:
When something fails at run time, C# **throws an exception**: an index out of range, a null reference, text that isn't a number. If nothing catches it, the program stops. `try`/`catch` lets you handle it:

```csharp
try
{
    int[] slots = new int[3];
    slots[5] = 1;                          // throws IndexOutOfRangeException
    Console.WriteLine("never printed");
}
catch (IndexOutOfRangeException ex)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Caught: {ex.Message}");
}
finally
{
    Console.WriteLine("finally always runs");
}
Console.WriteLine("the program carries on");
```

The rest of the `try` block is skipped as soon as the exception is thrown. `finally` runs whether or not anything went wrong, which makes it the place for clean-up.

