steps.body:
If bad input is *likely* (anything a person types), don't wait for an exception: use a **`TryParse`** method, which returns `true` or `false`. When **your own** code gets something it can't work with, `throw` an exception yourself so the mistake can't go unnoticed:

```csharp
string input = "42x";
if (int.TryParse(input, out int number))   // no exception: true or false
{
    Console.WriteLine(number * 2);
}
else
{
    Console.WriteLine($"'{input}' is not a number");   // this runs
}

Withdraw(10);                              // withdrew 10

void Withdraw(int amount)
{
    if (amount <= 0)
        throw new ArgumentException("amount must be positive");   // a real mistake: fail loudly
    Console.WriteLine($"withdrew {amount}");
}
```

Catch the **specific** exceptions you can handle. An empty `catch` that swallows everything hides real bugs. Unlike Java, C# never forces you to declare or catch an exception.

