steps.body:
An **event** is a delegate with a lock on it: code outside the class may only **subscribe** (`+=`) and **unsubscribe** (`-=`); only the owning class can **raise** it.

```csharp
var board = new ScoreBoard();
board.ScoreChanged += s => Console.WriteLine($"HUD shows {s}");
board.ScoreChanged += s => { if (s >= 20) Console.WriteLine("New record!"); };

board.Add(10);
board.Add(15);

class ScoreBoard
{
    public int Score { get; private set; }
    public event Action<int>? ScoreChanged;   // outsiders may only += and -=

    public void Add(int points)
    {
        Score += points;
        ScoreChanged?.Invoke(Score);          // ?. skips the call when nobody listens
    }
}
```

`ScoreBoard` never mentions the HUD or records: it just announces changes. This keeps parts of a game independent: the same idea is behind Unity's UI buttons and many game systems.

